# Prescription authoring authority and Blade PHP-directive pairing (rule 226)

**Status:** GO scope of `DMS-RME-REVISION-PROGRAM-8A` (2026-11-07). Sprint record:
`docs/sprints/dms-rme-revision-program-8a.md`. Owner approval: the system-owner mission brief of 2026-11-07 authorised
a corrective sprint for the Program 8 release blockers P8-N1 and P8-N3 and the critical-gate coverage gap, verified
defects only (CG-22). Category under the Foundation-First Sprint Lock: security fix / clinical correctness.

This rule EXTENDS rule 223 §3 (CW5-05) to the prescription. It supersedes nothing; rule 223 §3 still owns the
front-desk-only tier for the RME and the odontogram, and its §16 owner decisions are unchanged.

**Extended by rule 227 (`DMS-RME-REVISION-PROGRAM-8B`, 2026-11-07).** §1.3 below claimed a caller that skips the policy
is "refused the same way"; until 8B the service predicate did not re-check the write permission (P8R-S1), so the claim
was an overstatement. Rule 227 §3 adds the permission and a clinical-role allowlist to the predicate, which makes §1.3
true. Rule 227 §2 adds the printed dentist and patient names to §1.6's server-derived identity, and rule 227 §1 decides
the read-scope item §1.7 left open (P8-N2). Where the two rules differ, rule 227 wins.

## 1. A prescription is authored like the rest of the clinical record (P8-N1)

1. The prescription, its R/ canvas and its doctor-signature canvas are clinical documents printed under the visit
   doctor's name. Their write abilities (`RmePrescriptionPolicy::create`, `::update`) follow the clinical authoring
   authority, never `manage_clinic_visits` alone (that is the front office's registration permission, rule 223 §15).
2. `App\Modules\Prescription\Services\PrescriptionAuthoringAuthority` is the single definition:
   - the front-desk-only tier (`FrontOfficeRole::isVisitFrontDeskOnly()`) never writes a prescription;
   - a write is scoped to the actor's WORKING branch through `RmeWorkingBranchScope::allows()` — the per-record
     guard the visit's own write authority uses. Context-bound roles (Perawat, Admin Klinik, Kasir) write only in the
     branch they work in and fail closed without a context; every other actor — governance roles and Doctors alike —
     keeps the full active RME set (the per-record guard deliberately does not apply the doctor branch lock, exactly
     as `ClinicVisitPolicy` does; a Doctor is narrowed by the doctor-patient scope instead), so this only ever narrows;
   - the doctor-patient scope still binds a Doctor;
   - the actor is a usable account (`ActiveAccountResolver::isUsable()`).
3. The policy asks `permitsActorAtBranch()` and then `DoctorPatientScopeService::authorizeVisitAccess()` (which
   carries its own deny message). `RmePrescriptionService::create()` / `::update()` re-assert the whole predicate
   through `assertPermits()` before any lock is taken or any canvas is decoded or written (CG-06): a caller that never
   passes through the policy — a job, a command, a future controller — is refused the same way and leaves no object.
4. The predicate mirrors `RmeVisitConsentService::actorMayWorkOnEncounter()` on purpose and adds nothing that method's
   callers add: prescriptions carry NO consent prerequisite and NO room rule. Adding either is product policy.
5. The prescription editor on the page follows the same capability (`can('create', …)` already decides `canManage`),
   so the UI never offers a write the server refuses (rule 135). Reading a prescription is unchanged.
6. Identity of the record comes from the server: `created_by` / `updated_by` are the acting account, `doctor_id`,
   `patient_id` and `branch_id` come from the visit; request values for those fields are ignored.
7. NOT decided here (owner decisions, recorded in the sprint doc): whether Perawat / Supervisor RME may author
   prescriptions (rule 223 §16); prescription room isolation (OD-7-2); whether a prescription may be amended after its
   visit completed (OD-8A-1); a prescription audit trail (OD-8A-2); the read-scope hardening P8-N2 (read abilities are
   untouched here). `RmePrescriptionAuthoringAuthorityTest` pins today's answer for Perawat (RX-05), Supervisor RME
   (RX-06), OD-7-2 (RX-51) and OD-8A-1 (RX-50) so this rule is provably neutral on them.
7a. Known non-authoring write: the first print stamps `printed_at` (once; nothing else) through `markPrinted()`, under
    the read-level `print` ability. It is metadata, not authorship, and stays with the read abilities (P8-N2).

## 2. A Blade template never mixes the inline and the block PHP directive (P8-N3)

8. Blade stores raw PHP blocks before it compiles anything else AND before it strips comments, pairing the first PHP
   directive opener with the next block closer. A template that uses the inline (parenthesised) form above a block form
   therefore compiles to an open tag immediately followed by `(`, with the literal directive inside it.
9. What PHP does with that is decided by `short_open_tag`, not by the PHP version. With it On (the default when no
   php.ini is loaded: the CI image PHP 8.3.33 and the official 8.3.6 image) `<?` opens PHP and the literal directive
   is a ParseError (HTTP 500); with it Off (production's php.ini, and the workstation's PHP 8.5) the glued tag is not
   an open tag and the template source is printed as text with HTTP 200. Measured 2026-11-07: the same PHP 8.5 binary
   flips between the two with `-d short_open_tag`. The defect is in the template either way.
10. Use one PHP block per template (or only inline forms). Never write the directive tokens inside a Blade comment
    either: the raw-block pass sees them before comments are removed.
11. `tests/Feature/Ui/BladePhpDirectivePairingTest.php` compiles every template under `resources/views` with the real
    compiler and applies two checks, because neither alone covers every shape: an open tag glued to the next
    character (the only signal when `short_open_tag` is Off, where that shape parses), and a real parse of the compiled
    view (`token_get_all(…, TOKEN_PARSE)`, which catches a spaced inline opener and a closer hidden in a later Blade
    comment). 517 templates, 0 hits on either check under both settings at 2026-11-07; positive controls cover all three
    shapes. Do not weaken it into a source grep: it would miss a mis-pair created by a comment.

## 3. Critical-gate coverage (CI-8A)

12. The prescription suites and the ClinicMasterData suites run in the NSF-R011 critical gate: tokens `RmePrescription`
    (selects exactly `RmePrescriptionTest` + `RmePrescriptionAuthoringAuthorityTest`), `ClinicMasterData` (exactly the
    eight suites of `tests/Feature/ClinicMasterData`, zero unrelated files, measured with `--list-tests`) and
    `BladePhpDirectivePairing`, in BOTH runner variants, placed before the pinned calendar-boundary tail (rule 212,
    AIR-CIT-34). The security-critical suites are declared in `config/ci_runner.php` `critical_gate_mandatory_suites`
    (rule 120), so a rename fails the gate instead of dropping out.
